Avoid task id collisions across offline clones

Two clones working offline both pick the same next id and then both write their own tasks/<id> blob. Whoever pushes first wins; the other clone's task gets clobbered or fails the push lease and is stuck.

Approach: rebase-style id reassignment driven by creation timestamp.

Each task already has a `created` log entry with a unix timestamp. On `tsk git-pull`, after the existing reconciliation pass, scan for id collisions:
- For each colliding id N, compare local and remote `created` timestamps.
- The earlier task keeps id N. The later task gets reassigned to a new id allocated past the merged `next` counter.
- For each reassigned task, rewrite every reference to its old id:
  - body text containing `[[tsk-N]]`
  - attrs whose value matches the link form (source, assigned, parent, child-of, references, referenced-by, etc)
  - backlinks blobs of other tasks that referenced it
  - log entries (the `accepted`/`exported`/`links-changed` details)
  - the index ref's per-row id
- Append a `renumbered\t<old-id>\t<new-id>` log entry on the moved task so history is recoverable.

Push side: after the rebase, push proceeds normally (force-with-lease holds, our shadow now matches what we just pulled).

Edge cases to design for:
- Identical creation timestamps (resolution race): break ties by clone identity (could be `user.email` from git config) or by source ref OID lexicographic order.
- A reassigned task that someone has already accepted in another namespace: the `source` link in the destination namespace becomes stale. Could log a warning, or push an explicit rename event into the destination's inbox.
- External tools (other workspaces, foreign-link consumers) holding stale `[[ns/tsk-N]]` references — out of scope; document the renumber.

This integrates with the existing `tsk git-pull` reconciliation pass; should run as a final step after blob merges, before the local refs are advertised as the "new state".
